                 x) '(f 21)]))))\n1307\t    (testing (str \"raise through a parameter named \" binding)\n1308\t      (is (= [:yin.k/error {:yin.k/message \"boom\", :yin.k/data {:x 21}}]\n1309\t             (run-outcome\n1310\t               ['(defn g\n1311\t                   []\n1312\t                   (throw (ex-info \"boom\" {:x 21})))\n1313\t                (list 'defn 'f [binding] '(g))\n1314\t                '(+ 1 (f 5))])))))\n1315\t  (is (= [:yin.k/ok 1]\n1316\t         (run-outcome '[(def f (let [get 5] (fn [x] x))) (f 1)]))))\n1317\t\n1318\t\n1319\t(deftest test-common-call-source-admission\n1320\t  (doseq [form '[(let [x 5] (x 1))\n1321\t                 (let [x {:ordinary :map}] (x 1))\n1322\t                 (let [x {:yang.call/entry 5}] (x 1))\n1323\t                 (let [x {:py/type :function :code :legacy}] (x 1))\n1324\t                 (let [x {:yang.call/entry 5}] ((if true x x) 1))\n1325\t                 (let [x {:yang.call/entry (yang.call/entry f)}] (x 1))\n1326\t                 (let [x (assoc {} :yang.call/entry (get f :yang.call/entry))] (x 1))\n1327\t                 (let [x (assoc f :extra :forged)] (x 1))\n1328\t                 (let [x (assoc f :yang.call/entry (yang.call/entry g))] (x 1))\n1329\t                 (let [x (assoc {} :yang.call/entry (yang.call/entry f)\n1330\t                                :clj/admission (get f :clj/admission))] (x 1))\n1331\t                 (let [x {:yang.call/entry (yang.call/entry f)\n1332\t                          :clj/admission (fn [x] true)}] (x 1))]]\n1333\t    (doseq [wrap [identity #(list '+ 1 %)]]\n1334\t      (let [result (run-outcome ['(defn f\n1335\t                                    [x]\n1336\t                                    x)\n1337\t                                 '(defn g\n1338\t                                    [x]\n1339\t                                    (+ x 1)) (wrap form)])]\n1340\t        (is (= :yin.k/error (first result)) (pr-str form))\n1341\t        (is (= :yang.call/not-callable (:yang.call/reason (second result)))\n1342\t            (pr-str form)))))\n1343\t  (is (= [:yin.k/ok [false false true 21]]\n1344\t         (run-outcome '[(defn f\n1345\t                          [x]\n1346\t                          x)\n1347\t                        [(yang.call/eligible? {:yang.call/entry (yang.call/entry f)})\n1348\t                         (yang.call/eligible? (assoc f :extra :forged))\n1349\t                         (yang.call/eligible? f)\n1350\t                         (f 21)]]))))\n1351\t\n1352\t\n1353\t(deftest test-common-call-invalid-continuation-refuses\n1354\t  (let [tamper (fn [forms]\n1355\t                 (walk/postwalk\n1356\t                   (fn [node]\n1357\t                     (if (= node u/capture) (u/lit :invalid-continuation) node))\n1358\t                   (outcome-ast forms)))]\n1359\t    (doseq [run [walker-run semantic-run]]"}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_QVbk5Nqe69us55sHpvN5WENN","content":"3510\tchecking, and Ruby dynamic dispatch are plugin-owned stages. Each has\n3511\texplicit inputs, outputs, and dependencies. None requires Yang core to\n3512\trecognize its language by name.\n3513\t\n3514\t### 8.10 Shared function calling convention\n3515\t\n3516\t**Status: proposed, 2026-10-29; not implemented.** This section specifies\n3517\tone invocation convention for every conforming ANTLR-based Yang frontend.\n3518\tExamples are non-exhaustive. Adding a language must not add a case to Yang\n3519\tcore, a language-pair bridge, or an evaluator transition. The words\n3520\t**must**, **must not**, and **may** state requirements on a future\n3521\tconforming implementation, not claims about current code.\n3522\t\n3523\t#### 8.10.1 Decision and scope\n3524\t\n3525\tA Yin function is a Yin function, independent of its source syntax. The\n3526\tVM applies a closure to operands. Source calling semantics are ordinary\n3527\tUAST implementing the call site and callee entry, above the VM.\n3528\t\n3529\tEvery supported source-level callable, after its source-required\n3530\tresolution, exposes `:yang.call/entry`, an ordinary Yin closure taking\n3531\texactly one invocation record. Invoking it is an ordinary\n3532\t`:application`. No AST tag, lambda slot, instruction, host primitive,\n3533\teffect kind, or source-language test is added to the VM.\n3534\t\n3535\tThis is an internal calling convention, not a transport, another system\n3536\tABI, or a host callback interface. Calls between source languages inside\n3537\tone task do not themselves cross a host boundary. Portable I/O and\n3538\tcross-task interaction continue through `dao.stream`.\n3539\t\n3540\t**Transparent calling** means that an admitted callable can be passed,\n3541\tstored, returned, and invoked by any conforming frontend without a\n3542\thandwritten language-pair wrapper. It does not mean every foreign value\n3543\tsatisfies every source operation. Invocation, name visibility, and value\n3544\tinterpretation are distinct obligations.\n3545\t\n3546\tThe convention covers ordinary source calls and supported callable\n3547\tobjects, including higher-order calls. It does not make macros runtime\n3548\tfunctions, grant JavaScript `[[Construct]]`, turn a generator into an\n3549\tordinary returning function, or add a source feature a frontend refuses.\n3550\tConstructors and asynchronous operations keep their declared source\n3551\tsemantics; ordinary callability does not imply either capability.\n3552\t\n3553\t#### 8.10.2 Callable representation and identity\n3554\t\n3555\tKeep the callable's existing identity and source-specific data. Add the\n3556\tcommon entry beside its private code and metadata:\n3557\t\n3558\t```clojure\n3559\t;; Illustrative contents of one task-owned Python function cell.\n3560\t;; `entry` and `body-code` are live Yin closures, not literal AST maps.\n3561\t{:yang.call/entry entry\n3562\t :py/type :function\n3563\t :spec parameter-spec\n3564\t :defaults defaults\n3565\t :code body-code}\n3566\t```\n3567\t\n3568\tThe existing `:code` field is not the shared entry: its signature differs\n3569\tbetween preludes. It may remain private. Cross-language callers must not\n3570\tinspect it or extract it to bypass the entry.\n3571\t\n3572\tA cell-backed callable stores the entry in its cell contents. An\n3573\timmutable callable descriptor, such as a bound method, may carry it\n3574\tdirectly in its map. The common prelude projects the entry structurally:\n3575\tread an existing sealed cell reference through `cell/get`, or inspect\n3576\tthe immutable descriptor, then obtain `:yang.call/entry`. It must not\n3577\tdispatch on `:py/type`, `:js/type`, `:php/type`, language names, or a\n3578\tregistry of languages.\n3579\t\n3580\tAn admitted callable is a runtime cell/descriptor constructed or exposed\n3581\tby the callable constructors or import/export projections of a pinned,\n3582\tconforming profile. The entry slot is a runtime descriptor field, not a\n3583\tsource property that promotes arbitrary data to a function. A Python\n3584\tdictionary, JavaScript object, or PHP array containing a source key\n3585\tspelled `:yang.call/entry` remains ordinary source data unless an explicit\n3586\tprofile-defined projection makes it a callable descriptor. Source\n3587\tproperty access must not overwrite or masquerade as this runtime slot.\n3588\tThis is an obligation of construction and source resolution, not a new\n3589\tVM type, descriptor seal, or runtime provenance/language registry. Once\n3590\tthe resolved descriptor is admitted, the shared projection is structural;\n3591\tUAST deliberately constructing a conforming descriptor opts into the\n3592\tconvention rather than changing a source language's data-map semantics.\n3593\t\n3594\tMissing entry means not callable. Constructors must install a genuine\n3595\tunary Yin closure, and supported source mutations must preserve that\n3596\tinvariant. Unary arity is a constructor/profile conformance obligation,\n3597\tnot a signature introspection operation: Yin closures are opaque and the\n3598\tVM's existing argument binding does not enforce this convention's arity.\n3599\tA key in a map grants no VM privilege; application, ownership, and\n3600\teffect checks still apply. A non-applicable entry remains an operational\n3601\tVM application defect, not a source exception caught by the shared helper.\n3602\t\n3603\tFor callable kinds whose eligibility can change, the same cell or\n3604\tdescriptor may also contain `:yang.call/eligible?`, a unary Yin predicate\n3605\ton the original callable. Without it, an entry is eligible by default.\n3606\tWith it, the common callable test uses its boolean result. The predicate\n3607\treads explicit runtime metadata/heap only; it must not mutate state,\n3608\tevaluate arguments, execute the target body, or invoke getters/user hooks.\n3609\tIt has the same constructor/arity obligations as the entry. For example,\n3610\tan instance can keep one dispatch entry while eligibility follows whether\n3611\tits class currently supplies a supported call method; changing the class\n3612\tdoes not require rewriting every instance.\n3613\t\n3614\tCall eligibility is tested at the callability-check point required by\n3615\tthe caller's source, not universally before argument evaluation. The\n3616\tentry resolves/rechecks a dynamic target at its source-required dispatch\n3617\tpoint so argument side effects that change eligibility remain visible.\n3618\tStatic callable kinds need no predicate.\n3619\t\n3620\tSource callable designators, such as PHP function-name strings and\n3621\tcallable arrays where supported, are resolved under their source's rules\n3622\tbefore common invocation. They do not gain entry slots merely by being\n3623\tstrings or arrays. Exporting a designator as a first-class callable needs\n3624\tan explicit frontend projection to an admitted descriptor, retaining any\n3625\tsource-required late lookup and receiver semantics. Passing the raw\n3626\tstring or array as data leaves it unchanged; a foreign caller must not\n3627\tguess its language or search an ambient namespace to invoke it. This is\n3628\tsource resolution/projection, not a language-pair wrapper around an\n3629\talready admitted function.\n3630\t\n3631\tCross-language passage must not allocate a replacement function cell.\n3632\tReturned functions retain their original identity, captures, defaults,\n3633\tbound receiver, properties, and static state. A receiving frontend's\n3634\tcallable test follows this common eligibility rule rather than rejecting\n3635\tforeign native tags. Foreign attribute access and type reflection remain\n3636\tprofile-owned; callability grants no general right to interpret another\n3637\truntime's cell layout.\n3638\t\n3639\tCompiler-internal lambdas and host functions do not acquire this\n3640\tconvention merely by being applicable in Yin. A source import exposing\n3641\tone must emit an entry from its declared signature and effect profile.\n3642\tIt must not guess an opaque closure's signature or bypass host-effect\n3643\tand custody rules. Internal helper calls need not construct records.\n3644\t\n3645\t#### 8.10.3 Invocation record\n3646\t\n3647\tThe invocation is an ephemeral in-machine value. These keys are proposed\n3648\tvocabulary, not existing repository APIs:\n3649\t\n3650\t```clojure\n3651\t{:yang.call/callee     f\n3652\t :yang.call/positional [v0 v1 ...]\n3653\t :yang.call/named      [[\"name\" value] ...]\n3654\t :yang.call/raise      raise-exit\n3655\t ;; Optional: presence means a receiver was supplied, even when nil.\n3656\t :yang.call/receiver   receiver\n3657\t ;; Optional: pinned argument-binding extension, for example locations.\n3658\t :yang.call/binding-profile \"<content-address>\"}\n3659\t```\n3660\t\n3661\tThe four required fields mean:\n3662\t\n3663\t- `:yang.call/callee`: the original resolved callable, including its\n3664\t  identity. The invocation helper supplies it; an extracted entry must\n3665\t  not be applied to another object's substituted state.\n3666\t- `:yang.call/positional`: a dense ordered vector of evaluated values.\n3667\t  Its length records presence; no missing-argument sentinel is inserted.\n3668\t- `:yang.call/named`: ordered name/value pairs, not a map. Names are\n3669\t  plain text without caller-side case folding or normalization.\n3670\t  Duplicate names remain visible until the callee's binder decides them.\n3671\t- `:yang.call/raise`: this activation's caller-supplied, non-returning\n3672\t  guest exit, accepting the condition in section 8.10.7. It closes over\n3673\t  the caller's continuation and exception machinery, never a host\n3674\t  callback or hidden global handler slot.\n3675\t\n3676\tAn absent receiver differs from one whose value is `nil`, `:py/None`,\n3677\t`:php/null`, `:js/null`, or `:js/undefined`. Empty argument vectors are\n3678\trequired rather than represented by absent keys. Presence must not be\n3679\tinferred from truthiness.\n3680\t\n3681\tThe optional binding profile pins additional argument semantics. It\n3682\tdoes not name the caller's language or select a language-pair bridge.\n3683\tAbsent that extension, arguments are by-value runtime values as in\n3684\tsection 8.10.6. The callee entry compares the supplied profile address\n3685\twith its declared accepted/required binding profiles before acquiring\n3686\treferences or entering the body. An unsupported required profile or a\n3687\tmissing required extension fails there. The pinned profile defines the\n3688\tshape and validation of any extra invocation fields it requires; values\n3689\tin the base vectors alone do not establish a location protocol.\n3690\t\n3691\tThe PHP reference-argument profile is pinned as\n3692\t`sha256:171656777d777865312132fe18cbc7a057a4721844a4dc450c0800381d2223eb`.\n3693\tPHP call sites include it when their declared target has reference\n3694\tparameters; corresponding positional values may then be PHP reference\n3695\tmarkers for caller locations. The PHP entry rejects unknown profile\n3696\taddresses before parameter binding, and only this profile authorizes its\n3697\tbinder to attach a parameter to a caller marker. The markers alone carry\n3698\tno binding authority.\n3699\t\n3700\tConvention revision is pinned by the existing lowering/runtime profiles.\n3701\tIt is not a new canonical AST slot, a per-function language tag, or a\n3702\trepeated version field in every invocation. Incompatible revisions need\n3703\tnew profiles and explicit admission, never entry-shape guessing.\n3704\t\n3705\tThe shared helper validates the record's map shape, required presence,\n3706\tpositional vector, and ordered pairs with text names. The callee entry\n3707\tvalidates extension fields under its admitted binding profile; the shared\n3708\thelper knows no extension-specific representation. A valid caller-owned\n3709\traise exit is established before constructing the record, so structural\n3710\tfailures can be transferred through it. If no valid raise exit exists,\n3711\tthat is an operational wiring failure; do not invent a source exception\n3712\tpath, default missing fields to `nil`, or broadly catch VM application\n3713\terrors. Checking live entry/exit/predicate closure arity is not part of\n3714\tthis validation; constructor fixtures and admission under pinned\n3715\tprofiles establish those obligations.\n3716\t\n3717\t#### 8.10.4 Source semantics at caller and callee\n3718\t\n3719\tThe caller's lowering preserves its source rules for callable resolution,\n3720\targument/receiver evaluation, and spread or unpacking. Evaluate each\n3721\texpression exactly once, in the source-defined order. Errors required\n3722\tbefore argument evaluation must still occur before it. The record holds\n3723\tevaluated values; the callee must not reevaluate source expressions.\n3724\t\n3725\tAfter source resolution, calls use the common projection and application.\n3726\tFor a cell-backed callable, the successful path is schematically:\n3727\t\n3728\t```clojure\n3729\t;; UAST notation after source evaluation/resolution and validation.\n3730\t;; The caller has already established `raise-exit`.\n3731\t(let [entry (get (cell/get f) :yang.call/entry)\n3732\t      invocation (assoc evaluated-arguments\n3733\t                        :yang.call/callee f\n3734\t                        :yang.call/raise raise-exit)]\n3735\t  (entry invocation))\n3736\t```\n3737\t\n3738\tThe shared prelude handles projection and validation for cell-backed and\n3739\timmutable callables. Existing `py/call`, `js/call`, or `php/call` names\n3740\tmay remain as source call-site helpers, but must not reject an admitted\n3741\tforeign callable because its native type tag differs.\n3742\t\n3743\tThe callee entry installs its exception boundary **before parameter\n3744\tbinding**. It owns defaults, arity checks, positional/named/variadic\n3745\tbinding, receiver interpretation, and supported source constraints and\n3746\tcoercions. It then invokes its body using its private lowered signature\n3747\tand captured environment.\n3748\t\n3749\tEntry boilerplate is not an additional source function activation.\n3750\tSource recursion limits, tracing, and safepoint call marks must count the\n3751\toriginal source activation once, not both the entry and its private body\n3752\tas two source calls. Genuine nested source calls remain observable.\n3753\t\n3754\t| Callee example | Interpretation of the same invocation |\n3755\t|---|---|\n3756\t| Python function | Python binding/default/keyword rules and captured module globals. A missing required argument raises the Python binding error. |\n3757\t| JavaScript function | Its receiver mode and argument/default/rest rules. An absent argument yields `undefined` where specified. Nonempty named arguments raise a guest `TypeError`; they are not discarded or converted to a positional object. |\n3758\t| PHP function | Named-parameter, static-state, reference and declared type rules. Function self comes from `:yang.call/callee`, not the JavaScript receiver. |\n3759\t| Further frontend | Its declared binding rules, with no new case in the shared prelude or evaluator. |"}
