{"role":"assistant","content":"## Ruling: **D — token-record lane with one record construction**\n\nNone of the measured A/B/C candidates currently satisfies all constraints.\n\n**Reject C.** Publishing the raw body under `:clj/body` makes it guest-reachable. Descriptors are ordinary guest maps, and source code can already extract descriptor closures with `get` (`test/yang/clojure_test.clj:1326-1330`). Thus C permits the equivalent of:\n\n```clojure\n((get f :clj/body) [42])\n```\n\nThat bypasses the published entry and its admission/outcome boundary. This directly violates the convention: private body code “may remain private,” but callers “must not inspect it or extract it to bypass the entry” (`docs/design/yang.antlr.md:3568-3570`). Raising the UCF visit bound or adding VM memoization cannot repair that authenticity defect.\n\n### Approved implementation\n\nKeep the restored **strictly unary public entry**, retain static `intra-unit-fn?` routing, and have the generated intra-unit helper:\n\n1. Resolve `:yang.call/entry`.\n2. Construct the authenticated record with **one** seven-argument `dsg__assoc` call:\n\n   ```clojure\n   (dsg__assoc {}\n               :yang.call/positional dsg__args\n               :yang.call/named []\n               :clj/outcome-token dsg__call_token)\n   ```\n\n3. Apply the public entry once.\n\n`dsg__assoc` already accepts three key/value pairs in one invocation (`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2220-2236`). Design B’s three separate closure calls were therefore not an inherent cost of token authentication. The record needs neither `callee` nor `raise`: this path applies the entry directly and uses the token-authenticated outcome-forwarding lane.\n\nThis preserves:\n\n- unary publication;\n- no body closure or private selector on the descriptor;\n- compiler-held token authentication;\n- existing dynamic admission for first-class, copied, forged, and cross-boundary calls;\n- tail/non-tail outcome behavior and operand evaluation order.\n\n### Budgets and runtime\n\n- Keep the **2M instruction budget**.\n- Keep the slice-8 machine-data bound at **100,000**; do not recalibrate it to 250,000.\n- **Do not authorize `yin.vm` identity memoization** or other shared-runtime changes.\n- HEAD `0dc54289` remains reference-only; do not land its flag lane.\n\nThe single-construction D variant still needs measurement; its 2M acceptance must not be claimed before the focused gate completes.\n\n### Required review\n\nCompiler/authenticity re-review plus the normal quality gate, including:\n\n- focused gate: both fixtures complete at 2M on both lanes, with `102/2/2` and the expected 20 unexercised arities;\n- explicit proof that `:clj/body` is absent and no extracted private body is callable;\n- existing forged/copied descriptor, shadowing, redefinition, error-outcome, and operand-order tests;\n- tail and non-tail intra-unit regressions;\n- slice-8 reconstruction at the unchanged 100,000-visit bound;\n- compiler suite and exhaustion-consumer verification.\n\nNo shared-runtime review is needed because this ruling changes only `yang.clojure` lowering and its tests. If D still misses 2M, return the measurements; neither C nor a budget increase is an approved fallback."}
